ballfix - stops false double clicks of a trackball, and changes buttons
======================================================================

SYNOPSIS

    ballfix [-v] [-d DEVICE] [-t MS] [-m FROM=TO] ...

DESCRIPTION

The Kensington SlimBlade Pro (USB 047d:80d7) has fixed firmware that
cannot be flashed, unlike the Moonlander's. A worn switch bounces: one
press reaches the computer as press, release, press. ballfix reads the
ball, and passes everything on through a new device named "ballfix".

A release is held back for MS milliseconds. If the same button is
pressed again within that time, the release and the press are both
thrown away: the button stays down, as your finger kept it. A click
costs MS ms of delay on its release, nothing on its press.

OPTIONS

    -d DEVICE  the ball's event device (default: its name under
               /dev/input/by-id, "-event-mouse")
    -t MS      the time above (default 40; 0 turns it off). Raise it if
               false clicks go on, lower it if fast real clicks are lost.
    -m FROM=TO button FROM acts as button TO. Names: left right middle
               side extra forward back task. May be given many times.
    -v         say on stderr every button event, every change and every
               bounce. Use it to learn which name is which button.

It grabs the ball, so the system sees only the new device. It needs
access to /dev/uinput and to the ball's event file (groups plover and
input here). When it ends, the ball is released.

EXAMPLE

    ballfix -v                       find the names of the buttons
    ballfix -t 60 -m side=middle &   side button clicks the middle

BUILD

    make; make install PREFIX=~/.system   (or ./install.sh ballfix)
